- Can't Stop probability and strategy analysis
    Summary: Can't Stop analysis: four dice flatten odds, middle columns dominate, consistent probabilistic strategies win, design favors playability. (¶1, ¶11, ¶20, ¶24)
        - 1 Roll four dice, pair freely, advance three active markers; bust loses the turn's progress. (¶2, ¶3)
            - 1.1 Evidence: Columns 2-12 have lengths 3-5-7-9-11-13-11-9-7-5-3; complete three columns to win. (¶3)
            - 1.2 Forced-move rule: chosen pairing forces ALL its legal moves, even unwanted ones. (¶5)
            - 1.3 Example: (1,1,3,4) as 2+7 on {6,7,8} forces taking both 2 and 7. (¶5)
        - 2 Two-dice baseline peaks at 7 with 16.67%, exactly 6x likelier than 2 at 2.78%. (¶6)
            - 2.1 Evidence: Sums 2-12 have 1-2-3-4-5-6-5-4-3-2-1 ways of 36; 1 way versus 6 ordered ways. (¶6, ¶7)
            - 2.2 Nuance: Column 7 is only about 4.3x longer (13/3) than column 2, not 6x. (¶7)
        - 3 Four-dice pairing offers three pairings and sharply flattens the distribution. (¶8, ¶11)
            - 3.1 Evidence: P(can make 2) = 171/1296 approx 13.2%, up from two-dice 2.78%. (¶9)
            - 3.2 Evidence: P(can make 7) = 834/1296 approx 64.4% via inclusion-exclusion; overlaps 24/1296, triple 0. (¶10)
            - 3.3 Evidence: Full range 13.2% to 64.4%; probability ratio 4.88 nearly matches length ratio 4.33. (¶11)
        - 4 Three-column combination choice dominates single-column odds. (¶13, ¶14)
            - 4.1 Evidence: Expected rolls L/P: column 2 needs 22.73, column 7 needs 20.18 rolls. (¶12)
            - 4.2 Evidence: {6,7,8} succeeds 1193/1296 = 92.0% with Q 1.43 markers per roll. (¶13, ¶14)
            - 4.3 Evidence: {2,3,12} succeeds only 568/1296 = 43.8% with Q 1.05; bust likelier. (¶13, ¶14)
            - 4.4 Evidence: All 165 combos ranked; 37 excellent at 85%+, median 79.6%, worst 43.8%. (¶14)
            - 4.5 Nuance: Clean moves only: 39.8% for {6,7,8} and 2.3% for {2,3,12}. (¶14, ¶16)
        - 5 Keep rolling while expected gain exceeds expected loss. (¶15, ¶16)
            - 5.1 Evidence: Rule: continue if (P_success x Q) > (P_bust x U); {6,7,8} gives 1.32 > 0.4. (¶15)
            - 5.2 Evidence: {2,3,12} with 2 steps gives 1.12 > 0.46 loss, so stop. (¶16)
            - 5.3 Nuance: Continuation usually beats switching, but full quantitative comparison remains open future work. (¶17)
        - 6 Tournament of 38 strategies across 14 families over 3.6M games crowns FiftyPercentSurvival. (¶18, ¶20)
            - 6.1 Evidence: Scale: 1,000 trials per strategy, 2,500 games per matchup, 3.6M games total. (¶18, ¶20)
            - 6.2 Evidence: Fastest GreedyUntil1Col 10.5 turns (median 9, sd 5.73, 7.50 busts); top 15 span 10.5-12.8. (¶19)
            - 6.3 Evidence: Champion FiftyPercentSurvival 69.84% (132,696/190,000); Heuristic(1.5) 66.46% second. (¶20)
            - 6.4 Evidence: Champion stops when cumulative survival drops below 50%: n = log(0.5)/log(P_success). (¶21)
            - 6.5 Nuance: Paradox: GreedyUntil1Col #1 speed but #11 at 58.90%; consistency beats speed. (¶20)
            - 6.6 Evidence: First-player advantage +11.18% overall (P1 wins 55.59%); GreedyUntil1Col bias +31.24%. (¶20)
            - 6.7 Nuance: Opponent-aware tweaks did not help; web game plus full code and data released. (¶4, ¶26)
        - 7 Board fits 1980 constraints; forced-move rule compensates residual imbalance. (¶22, ¶23)
            - 7.1 Evidence: Single-column deviations stay within +/-13.6% given integer lengths. (¶22)
            - 7.2 Evidence: Perfect balance needs 171 vs 834 steps, about an 8-hour game. (¶22)
            - 7.3 Evidence: Optimal +-1-step board [3,5,8,10,12,14,12,10,8,5,3] cuts deviation to +/-3.09%, 4x better. (¶22)
            - 7.4 Evidence: Best under-20 board [4,7,11,14,17,20,17,14,11,7,4] hits +/-2.17%; integer linear design +/-3.32%. (¶23)
            - 7.5 Nuance: Advice: play 50% rule on columns 5-9; designers prioritize playability plus handicaps. (¶24, ¶25)
